Two pseudo-classes on the same compound selector (`:hover`, `:focus`,
`:active`) have EQUAL specificity ((0,2,0) each), so only the sheet
position decides between them. Each block's class list (its
`[@@@css.blocks]` entry, which the ppx writes) orders them as the block's
source does.

Two library modules style the same kind of element the same way: module
`aaa` (read first - alphabetically before `zzz`) writes `:focus` before
`:hover` (its own intent: hover overrides focus); module `zzz` writes the
opposite (its own intent: focus overrides hover). Both use the SAME two
atoms (same declarations, so the same classes - the common case: two
components sharing one "danger" hover/focus color reuse the same atomized
rule).

  $ cat > aaa_focus_then_hover.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css "._a_2focusclr:focus{color:blue;}"]
  > [@@@css "._a_1hoverclr:hover{color:red;}"]
  > [@@@css.blocks [(3, "_a_2focusclr _a_1hoverclr")]]
  > EOF

  $ cat > zzz_hover_then_focus.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css "._a_1hoverclr:hover{color:red;}"]
  > [@@@css "._a_2focusclr:focus{color:blue;}"]
  > [@@@css.blocks [(3, "_a_1hoverclr _a_2focusclr")]]
  > EOF

One sheet can not satisfy both blocks. generate warns, names both blocks
(`file:line` of the class list, since the inputs carry no binding names),
and keeps definition order for the pair: `aaa` is read first, so its order
wins in both CLI orders:

  $ styled-ppx.generate aaa_focus_then_hover.ml zzz_hover_then_focus.ml
  styled-ppx: blocks aaa_focus_then_hover.ml:3 and zzz_hover_then_focus.ml:3 put two competing rules in opposite orders (._a_2focusclr:focus{color:blue;} and ._a_1hoverclr:hover{color:red;}); the sheet keeps their definition order
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  ._a_2focusclr:focus{color:blue;}
  ._a_1hoverclr:hover{color:red;}

  $ styled-ppx.generate zzz_hover_then_focus.ml aaa_focus_then_hover.ml
  styled-ppx: blocks aaa_focus_then_hover.ml:3 and zzz_hover_then_focus.ml:3 put two competing rules in opposite orders (._a_2focusclr:focus{color:blue;} and ._a_1hoverclr:hover{color:red;}); the sheet keeps their definition order
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  ._a_2focusclr:focus{color:blue;}
  ._a_1hoverclr:hover{color:red;}

An element of `zzz` that is hovered and focused at once gets `red`, against
its own source order: the conflict warning names it.

A pair that only one block orders has no conflict: the block decides, even
against definition order.

  $ cat > aaa_active_then_hover.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css "._a_6activebg:active{background:green;}"]
  > [@@@css "._a_5hoverbg:hover{background:red;}"]
  > EOF

  $ cat > zzz_hover_then_active.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css.blocks [(1, "_a_5hoverbg _a_6activebg")]]
  > EOF

  $ styled-ppx.generate zzz_hover_then_active.ml aaa_active_then_hover.ml
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  ._a_5hoverbg:hover{background:red;}
  ._a_6activebg:active{background:green;}
